Development environments require governance just like production environments, although their requirements may differ. Without controls, developers can unintentionally create large numbers of expensive or insecure resources.

Development governance can include:

* Approved regions
* Spending limits
* Resource tags
* Automatic shutdown schedules
* Restricted resource types
* Appropriate RBAC permissions
* Cleanup policies

For example, development virtual machines can be automatically shut down outside business hours to prevent unnecessary consumption.

A real-life use case is a software company giving developers flexibility to experiment while maintaining cost and security controls.

Effective development governance enables innovation without allowing uncontrolled cloud consumption.
